WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:07.09
This is the first slice of journey one in Hewn: an Owner submits a product direction,

00:00:07.09 --> 00:00:12.22
and Hewn creates the project, queues the direction and opens the project page.

00:00:12.22 --> 00:00:16.63
Everything after this moment runs without asking a person anything,

00:00:16.63 --> 00:00:20.32
so this is the one screen where a human gives direction.

00:00:21.47 --> 00:00:24.79
The slice runs top to bottom through the stack.

00:00:24.79 --> 00:00:30.74
In Mission Control, the New direction page takes a name, an idea, an optional stack,

00:00:30.74 --> 00:00:33.78
an optional GitHub repository and a budget.

00:00:33.78 --> 00:00:37.67
Behind it, POST /api/projects validates the submission,

00:00:37.67 --> 00:00:41.14
checks that Hewn can write to a named repository,

00:00:41.14 --> 00:00:45.81
and saves the project and its first direction in one unit of work.

00:00:46.16 --> 00:00:51.03
There was no code in the repository yet, so this pull request also lays the

00:00:51.03 --> 00:00:55.59
foundations journey one needs: the .NET solution with its four layers,

00:00:55.59 --> 00:01:00.72
cookie sessions against the allow list, the event store, the Angular workspace,

00:01:00.72 --> 00:01:03.65
the Playwright suite, continuous integration,

00:01:03.65 --> 00:01:06.45
and the video tooling that made this video.

00:01:07.60 --> 00:01:10.76
Here is the sequence from the detailed design.

00:01:10.76 --> 00:01:12.41
The page posts the form.

00:01:12.41 --> 00:01:16.94
The controller checks the Owner policy and the anti-forgery token,

00:01:16.94 --> 00:01:19.14
then hands a command to MediatR.

00:01:19.14 --> 00:01:23.61
The validator applies the field rules, the handler probes GitHub,

00:01:23.61 --> 00:01:28.42
and only after the commit does the API answer with two oh one Created.

00:01:28.77 --> 00:01:33.40
That commit also writes the first event for the project, DirectionQueued,

00:01:33.40 --> 00:01:34.99
with sequence number one.

00:01:34.99 --> 00:01:39.95
Every later change the project page shows will follow it with the next number.

00:01:41.10 --> 00:01:43.37
The handler is short on purpose.

00:01:43.37 --> 00:01:49.35
If a repository URL was given, it asks the GitHub probe whether Hewn can push there,

00:01:49.35 --> 00:01:51.91
and stops with a field error if not.

00:01:51.91 --> 00:01:57.46
Then the domain creates the project with its product direction, eleven stages,

00:01:57.46 --> 00:01:59.10
and stage zero Pending.

00:01:59.45 --> 00:02:01.80
Field rules live in one validator.

00:02:01.80 --> 00:02:06.70
A name must be one to one hundred characters and unique, ignoring case.

00:02:06.70 --> 00:02:10.16
An idea must be ten to twenty thousand characters.

00:02:10.16 --> 00:02:14.58
Each rule carries two texts: the inline message under the field,

00:02:14.58 --> 00:02:17.41
and a shorter line for the error summary.

00:02:17.76 --> 00:02:19.51
The controller stays thin.

00:02:19.51 --> 00:02:22.75
It requires the Owner policy, sends the command,

00:02:22.75 --> 00:02:25.91
and returns Created with the project's address.

00:02:25.91 --> 00:02:30.56
A Viewer never reaches the handler: the policy answers four oh three.

00:02:31.71 --> 00:02:36.56
Every acceptance test file starts with the requirement it traces to.

00:02:36.56 --> 00:02:42.12
The backend tests run the real API on an in-memory SQLite database with a fake

00:02:42.12 --> 00:02:42.62
GitHub.

00:02:42.62 --> 00:02:48.46
Written before the endpoints existed, they failed with four oh four on every call.

00:02:48.81 --> 00:02:54.06
Once the endpoints were in, they found two real bugs: a stage loop that read the

00:02:54.06 --> 00:02:57.01
instance list instead of the stage catalogue,

00:02:57.01 --> 00:03:00.56
and a stage number Entity Framework tried to generate.

00:03:00.56 --> 00:03:03.97
With both fixed, all twenty five backend tests pass,

00:03:03.97 --> 00:03:07.25
alongside fourteen Playwright tests for behaviour,

00:03:07.25 --> 00:03:10.34
accessibility and visual parity with the mocks.

00:03:11.49 --> 00:03:12.95
Here is the real app.

00:03:12.95 --> 00:03:18.44
The Owner types a short idea and submits; the inline error explains the allowed

00:03:18.44 --> 00:03:18.93
length.

00:03:18.93 --> 00:03:24.56
They finish the idea, submit again, and land on the project page with every stage

00:03:24.56 --> 00:03:25.12
Pending.

00:03:25.47 --> 00:03:30.56
When something is wrong, the summary at the top names each problem and links to its

00:03:30.56 --> 00:03:35.15
field, and focus moves to it so a screen reader announces it straight away.

00:03:35.50 --> 00:03:40.81
If the repository belongs to someone who has not installed Hewn's GitHub App,

00:03:40.81 --> 00:03:44.81
the error says so and offers the way out: install the app,

00:03:44.81 --> 00:03:48.81
or leave the field empty and let Hewn create a repository.

00:03:49.16 --> 00:03:53.34
A Viewer who opens the page sees that they don't have permission,

00:03:53.34 --> 00:03:58.36
and the API refuses their post with four oh three, whatever the browser shows.

00:03:58.71 --> 00:04:01.81
On a phone the same form stacks into one column,

00:04:01.81 --> 00:04:05.24
and the project page follows the system's dark theme.

00:04:06.39 --> 00:04:12.50
One page, one endpoint, one unit of work: the project and its direction exist before

00:04:12.50 --> 00:04:13.66
the API answers.

00:04:14.01 --> 00:04:16.47
The foundations are in place for the next slice.

00:04:16.82 --> 00:04:23.12
Next, the pipeline itself: a worker leases the direction and runs stages zero to ten

00:04:23.12 --> 00:04:23.79
in order.
